## 1. Question

CW-002J found that a general positive doubly-stochastic single Markov chain can encode the arithmetic frontier of linear recurrence positivity at the stationary cutpoint.

The next question was:

> Does reversibility/self-adjoint spectral geometry remove that difficulty?

For discrete time, the answer is now essentially yes.

A 2025 result on positivity for real-eigenvalue matrices supplies the missing theorem.

For continuous time, the situation is materially different.

---

# 2. Reversible Markov chains have real spectrum

Let \(P\) be a finite irreducible Markov kernel with stationary law \(\pi>0\).

Reversibility means detailed balance:

\[
\pi_iP_{ij}=\pi_jP_{ji}.
\]

Let

\[
\Pi=\operatorname{diag}(\pi).
\]

Then

\[
S=\Pi^{1/2}P\Pi^{-1/2}
\]

is real symmetric.

Hence:

1. \(P\) is diagonalizable;
2. every eigenvalue of \(P\) is real;
3. the spectral representation has no genuinely complex rotational phases.

This is the classical reversible-chain spectral theorem.

---

# 3. Any rational halfspace observable is a real-root LRS

Take rational data:

- rational reversible \(P\);
- rational initial distribution \(\mu\);
- rational observable \(f\);
- rational threshold \(\lambda\).

Define

\[
a_t
=
\mu P^tf-\lambda.
\]

By Cayley–Hamilton, \(a_t\) is a rational linear recurrence sequence.

Because \(P\) is diagonalizable with real eigenvalues, after combining repeated equal eigenvalues it has the form

\[
\boxed{
a_t
=
c_0+\sum_{r=1}^d c_r\theta_r^t,
}
\]

where all \(\theta_r\in\overline{\mathbb Q}\cap\mathbb R\).

So every characteristic root relevant to the sequence is real algebraic.

Negative roots may cause parity oscillation, but there are no irrational complex phases.

---

# 4. 2025 collision: positivity for real-root LRS is decidable

De les Coves, Graf, Klingler, and Netzer prove in "Positive Moments Forever: Undecidable and Decidable Cases" (LAA 2025) that moment positivity is decidable for rational matrices with only real eigenvalues.

They explicitly state the corresponding consequence:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Positivity is decidable for LRS whose characteristic roots are all real.}
}
\]

This is arbitrary order.

Therefore the low-order frontier used in CW-002J is not the right frontier once reversibility is imposed.

---

# 5. Main discrete-time theorem

### Theorem CW-002K.1 — Reversible halfspace crossing is decidable in arbitrary dimension

Given rational finite reversible \(P\), rational \(\mu,f,\lambda\), it is decidable whether

\[
\exists t\in\mathbb N:
\mu P^tf<\lambda.
\]

### Proof

Set

\[
a_t=\mu P^tf-\lambda.
\]

By reversibility, \(a_t\) is a rational LRS with only real characteristic roots.

The real-root Positivity theorem decides whether

\[
a_t\ge0
\quad\forall t.
\]

Negating that answer decides whether some \(a_t<0\).

QED.

Likewise, replacing \(a_t\) by \(-a_t\) decides whether some value exceeds the threshold.

Therefore all strict halfspace crossing questions are decidable.

---

# 6. Exact equality is also decidable

Akshay, Balaji, Murhekar, Varma, and Vyas (STACS 2020) prove decidability of the Skolem problem for LRS whose characteristic roots are roots of real algebraic numbers:

\[
\alpha^d=r,
\qquad
r\in\overline{\mathbb Q}\cap\mathbb R.
\]

Every real algebraic root belongs to this class by taking \(d=1\).

Hence:

### Theorem CW-002K.2 — Reversible hyperplane hitting is decidable

For rational reversible \(P,\mu,f,\lambda\), it is decidable whether

\[
\boxed{
\exists t:
\mu P^tf=\lambda.
}
\]

Thus, for one rational reversible chain, exact hyperplane hitting and strict halfspace crossing are both decidable in arbitrary state dimension.

