# 8. What symmetry removes

For a nonreversible real matrix, a scalar orbit can contain terms

\[
r^t\cos(t\theta+\phi),
\]

coming from complex conjugate eigenvalues.

Arithmetic questions then depend on how the discrete orbit

\[
t\theta\pmod{2\pi}
\]

approaches special phases.

This is where Diophantine approximation enters many LRS problems.

A reversible chain is similar to a symmetric matrix, so all eigenvalues are real.

Its scalar orbit contains only

\[
c_r\theta_r^t.
\]

The difficult rotational torus disappears.

### CWR principle

\[
\boxed{
\text{reversibility removes phase arithmetic from a single discrete Markov orbit}.
}
\]

This is the geometric reason behind the decidability transfer.

---

# 9. Negative eigenvalues do not reintroduce the hard phase

A positive reversible stochastic matrix can still have negative eigenvalues.

Example:

\[
P=
\begin{pmatrix}
2/15&11/15&2/15\\
11/15&2/15&2/15\\
2/15&2/15&11/15
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

It is positive, symmetric, and doubly stochastic, with spectrum

\[
\{1,3/5,-3/5\}.
\]

The stationary deviation

\[
(P^t)_{11}-1/3
\]

alternates sign.

But the only phase is parity:

\[
(-3/5)^t=(-1)^t(3/5)^t.
\]

Parity is a finite arithmetic progression, not an irrational torus.

That distinction is algorithmically decisive.

---

# 10. One reversible generator versus switched reversible generators

The theorem above concerns one fixed \(P\).

Now let

\[
P_1,\ldots,P_m
\]

all be reversible with respect to the same stationary law \(\pi\).

Each is self-adjoint in \(L^2(\pi)\).

But generally

\[
P_iP_j
\]

is not self-adjoint unless the generators commute.

Indeed:

\[
(P_iP_j)^\ast=P_jP_i.
\]

Thus noncommuting switching destroys one-step reversibility at the product level.

The scalar word problem

\[
\mu P_{w_1}\cdots P_{w_k}f
\]

is therefore not reduced to a one-variable real-root LRS.

### Open frontier

\[
\boxed{
\text{single reversible generator: decidable;}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{finite switched reversible family: classification open in this lab.}
}
\]

A direct literature search did not locate a cutpoint undecidability/decidability theorem under the exact restriction "all stochastic generators symmetric/reversible."

No novelty or openness claim beyond that search is made.

