# 12. Continuous-time reversible chains

Let \(Q\) be a finite reversible continuous-time Markov generator.

Then

\[
\Pi^{1/2}Q\Pi^{-1/2}
\]

is symmetric.

Its eigenvalues are real and nonpositive:

\[
0=-\beta_0>-\beta_1\ge\cdots\ge-\beta_d.
\]

For a scalar observable,

\[
g(t)=\mu e^{tQ}f-\lambda
\]

has the form

\[
\boxed{
g(t)
=
c_0+\sum_{r=1}^d c_re^{-\beta_rt}.
}
\]

This is a real exponential polynomial with no trigonometric frequencies.

---

# 13. Discrete-time decidability does not automatically transfer to continuous time

The Continuous Skolem literature studies exactly zeros of functions generated by linear ODEs / exponential polynomials.

General decidability is open.

Chonev–Ouaknine–Worrell show conditional decidability of important bounded variants under Schanuel's conjecture and identify major transcendence-theoretic obstacles.

Dantam–Pouly likewise obtain conditional results for continuous-time reachability when the dynamics has only real eigenvalues.

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{real spectrum is sufficient for discrete LRS Positivity decidability,}
}
\]

but current general continuous-time reachability theory does not permit us to claim the analogous arbitrary-dimensional theorem merely from real spectrum.

The reversible CTMC subclass is narrower than a general real-spectrum LDS, so this is not a proof of hardness or openness for the exact subclass.

It is a warning that the discrete proof does not carry over.

The exact reversible-CTMC zero problem remains under collision audit in this program.

