# 14. An unconditional continuous-time island: commensurate spectrum

There is a clean exact special case.

Assume the nonzero decay rates span a one-dimensional rational vector space:

\[
\beta_r=\frac{m_r}{q}\beta,
\]

where

\[
m_r\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0},
\qquad
q\in\mathbb N,
\qquad
\beta>0.
\]

Set

\[
z=e^{-\beta t/q}.
\]

For \(t\in[0,\infty)\),

\[
z\in(0,1].
\]

Then

\[
e^{-\beta_rt}=z^{m_r},
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
g(t)=P(z)
}
\]

for an algebraic-coefficient polynomial

\[
P(z)=c_0+\sum_rc_rz^{m_r}.
\]

Thus all questions

\[
g(t)=0,
\quad
g(t)>0,
\quad
g(t)<0
\]

reduce to algebraic polynomial sign/root questions on the interval

\[
0<z\le1.
\]

### Theorem CW-002K.3 — Commensurate-spectrum reversible CTMC decidability

For a finite reversible CTMC whose decay rates are rationally commensurate, exact hyperplane hitting and strict halfspace crossing for algebraic scalar observables are decidable.

This mechanism is classical-adjacent: continuous-LTI literature already records decidability when diagonal exponents are rational/rational multiples of a common algebraic scale.

---

# 15. Exact example

Take the symmetric generator

\[
Q=
\begin{pmatrix}
-1&1&0\\
1&-2&1\\
0&1&-1
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Its spectrum is

\[
\{0,-1,-3\}.
\]

For initial state \(1\) and the state-\(1\) indicator,

\[
p_{11}(t)
=
\frac13
+
\frac12e^{-t}
+
\frac16e^{-3t}.
\]

Testing threshold \(1/2\):

\[
p_{11}(t)-\frac12
=
-\frac16+\frac12e^{-t}+\frac16e^{-3t}.
\]

With

\[
z=e^{-t},
\]

this becomes

\[
\boxed{
\frac16z^3+\frac12z-\frac16.
}
\]

The relevant root in \((0,1]\) is approximately

\[
z=0.3221853546,
\]

corresponding to

\[
t\approx1.132628264.
\]

The continuous problem has become ordinary polynomial root isolation.

